    "title": "Cloud-Based Load Balancing and SDN-Powered Adaptive Video Streaming",
    "thumbnail": "https://files.elfsightcdn.com/eafe4a4d-3436-495d-b748-5bdce62d911d/e69a8c18-97c9-45f8-9c3d-44918376fbc4/1748080299346.jpg",
    "description": "A pretty solid project where I combined cloud infrastructure and software-defined networking into something actually functional and measurable.",
    "long_description": "A pretty solid project where I combined cloud infrastructure and software-defined networking into something actually functional and measurable.\n\nSet up a fully automated load balancer on AWS using Docker, NGINX, PHP, and MySQL. The system handled backend traffic with zero downtime and showed an 18.6 percent increase in throughput under load. Wrote a script to deploy everything in one go, no manual setup, no surprises.\n\nOn the other side, I built a video streaming testbed using Mininet and DASH.js, controlled by an SDN controller (OpenDaylight). The player adapted to real-time network changes like packet loss and bandwidth drops. I customized the DASH player to track bitrate shifts, buffer levels, and resolution changes, all in real time.\n\nEverything was built from scratch. No templates. Every part was scripted and tested under real conditions.\n\nThis was about solving real infrastructure problems using the tools that power today’s internet. On to the next!\n\n🧰 𝗧𝗲𝗰𝗵 𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗰𝗸 𝗶𝗻𝗰𝗹𝘂𝗱𝗲𝗱: 𝗔𝗪𝗦 𝗘𝗖𝟮, 𝗗𝗼𝗰𝗸𝗲𝗿 𝗖𝗼𝗺𝗽𝗼𝘀𝗲, 𝗔𝗽𝗮𝗰𝗵𝗲𝗕𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗵, 𝗡𝗚𝗜𝗡𝗫 𝗿𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗿𝘀𝗲 𝗽𝗿𝗼𝘅𝘆, 𝗢𝗽𝗲𝗻𝗗𝗮𝘆𝗹𝗶𝗴𝗵𝘁 𝗰𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗿, 𝗠𝗶𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗲𝘁 𝘁𝗼𝗽𝗼𝗹𝗼𝗴𝘆 𝘀𝗰𝗿𝗶𝗽𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴, 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗗𝗔𝗦𝗛.𝗷𝘀, 𝗟𝗶𝗻𝘂𝘅, 𝗨𝗯𝘂𝗻𝘁𝘂\n\nWanna know the full story? Click here! 👇🏻😀",
