  {
    "title": "Penetration Testing Simulation for Securing Organizational Systems",
    "thumbnail": "https://files.elfsightcdn.com/eafe4a4d-3436-495d-b748-5bdce62d911d/9960a94c-7450-466d-b278-30d6670329eb/WhatsApp-Image-2024-11-30-at-17-24-45.jpg",
    "description": "Conducted an in-depth penetration test and vulnerability assessment on a simulated organizational infrastructure using tools like OpenVAS, Nmap, Metasploit, and Armitage. Delivered a detailed report highlighting critical vulnerabilities, potential attack vectors, and actionable mitigation strategies. Skills include vulnerability assessment, web application testing (OWASP), and network traffic analysis",
    "long_description": "Conducted an in-depth penetration test and vulnerability assessment on a simulated organizational infrastructure using tools like OpenVAS, Nmap, Metasploit, and Armitage. Delivered a detailed report highlighting critical vulnerabilities, potential attack vectors, and actionable mitigation strategies. Skills include vulnerability assessment, web application testing (OWASP), and network traffic analysis. \n\nThis project was part of the PUSL3132 Ethical Hacking coursework and focused on conducting a thorough penetration test on Clarke’s Ceylon Team’s network infrastructure. The goal was to simulate real-world cyberattacks, identify vulnerabilities, and recommend actionable mitigations to enhance the organization's security posture.\n\nKey Objectives:\nVulnerability Identification: Detect and analyze weaknesses in the target network.\nExploit Simulation: Simulate real-world cyberattacks using ethical hacking tools and techniques.\nRisk Assessment: Evaluate the business and operational impact of identified vulnerabilities.\nMitigation Strategies: Propose technical and organizational measures to eliminate vulnerabilities.\nEthical Compliance: Ensure all activities adhered to strict ethical and legal standards.\nTesting Methodology: The penetration testing followed a structured methodology:\nPlanning and Reconnaissance:\nDefined the scope and legal permissions.\nUsed tools like Nmap for active reconnaissance to identify open ports and services.\nWHOIS and other utilities were used to gather network-specific details.\nScanning and Vulnerability Assessment:\nOpenVAS was employed to identify critical vulnerabilities such as outdated SMB protocols and remote code execution issues (e.g., CVE-2006-349 and CVE-2010-1301).\nAnalyzed service configurations and identified potential exploit vectors.\nExploitation:\nUtilized the Metasploit Framework to exploit vulnerabilities like EternalBlue (MS17-010).\nSuccessfully compromised the target system and gained administrative access via a Meterpreter session.\nExtracted password hashes and simulated post-exploitation techniques.\nRisk Evaluation:\nCategorized vulnerabilities based on likelihood, impact, and overall risk level.\nHigh-risk vulnerabilities included remote code execution and SMB misconfigurations.\nMitigation and Recommendations:\nTechnical fixes: Immediate patching, endpoint security, and strong access controls.\nPolicy and training: Developed security awareness programs and incident response plans.\nContinuous monitoring: Recommended the implementation of IDS/IPS and regular audits.\nKey Findings:\nCritical vulnerabilities such as remote code execution via SMB protocols.\nWeak password policies, including the use of default and easily guessable credentials.\nOutdated software and misconfigured services exposed the network to significant risks.\nHigh-priority vulnerabilities included CVE-2006-349 and CVE-2010-1301, which could lead to system compromise and data breaches.\nChallenges Overcome:\nEnsured strict adherence to the scope and ethical guidelines.\nWorked with limited resources and simulated environments.\nConducted in-depth analysis despite challenges in configuring testing tools.\nOutcome: The project provided Clarke’s Ceylon Team with a detailed vulnerability assessment, risk evaluation, and a roadmap for strengthening their network security. This experience enhanced practical skills in penetration testing, ethical hacking, and cybersecurity analysis while demonstrating the ability to address real-world security challenges.\n\nSkills Demonstrated:\nProficiency in penetration testing tools like Nmap, OpenVAS, and Metasploit.\nAnalytical skills for identifying vulnerabilities and assessing risks.\nEthical hacking principles and adherence to legal standards.\nReport writing and communication of technical findings to stakeholders.\nThis project highlights my ability to conduct comprehensive penetration tests and provide actionable recommendations to enhance organizational security.",
    "skills": [
      "PENTEST",
      "RED TEAM",
      "VULN ASSESSMENT"
    ],
    "link": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1su0VYpCo_uA5Cqt72cM-U48rPjYZEWlj/view?usp=share_link",
